deeper_reasoning_3:
\textbf{Claim.}
For the two-dimensional target set \(S^{[12]}\), the reported projected ranks from \(S^{[14]}\), \(S^{[16]}\), and \(S^{[18]}\) form a finite-chain saturation certificate:
\[
P_{14}=P_{16}=P_{18},\qquad
\dim I(2,S^{[12]},S^{[18]})=2,\qquad
d_{\mathrm{projected}}=66.
\]
The certificate is deliberately finite: it proves saturation along the tested nested chain, not over every possible larger auxiliary superset.

\textbf{Premises.}
\[
\begin{array}{ll}
(P1) & S^{[12]}\subset S^{[14]}\subset S^{[16]}\subset S^{[18]}.\\
(P2) & P_k:=P(2,S^{[12]},S^{[k]})\text{ for }k\in\{14,16,18\}.\\
(P3) & P_{14}\subseteq P_{16}\subseteq P_{18}.\\
(P4) & \dim P_{14}=\dim P_{16}=\dim P_{18}=53.\\
(P5) & \dim R_{\mathrm{dir}}(2,S^{[12]})=51.\\
(P6) & |S^{[12]}|=119.\\
(P7) & R_{\mathrm{dir}}(2,S^{[12]})\subseteq P_{18}.\\
(P8) & \text{If }U\subseteq V\text{ and }\dim U=\dim V,\text{ then }U=V.
\end{array}
\]

\textbf{Derivation.}
The nested auxiliary spaces give nested projected row spaces by (P3). Equal dimensions then force equality:
\begin{align}
P_{14}\subseteq P_{16}
\quad\text{and}\quad
\dim P_{14}=\dim P_{16}
&\Longrightarrow P_{14}=P_{16}
&&\text{by (P4), (P8)},\\
P_{16}\subseteq P_{18}
\quad\text{and}\quad
\dim P_{16}=\dim P_{18}
&\Longrightarrow P_{16}=P_{18}
&&\text{by (P4), (P8)}.
\end{align}
Thus
\[
P_{14}=P_{16}=P_{18}.
\]
The indirect quotient dimension at the largest tested auxiliary space is
\begin{align}
\dim I(2,S^{[12]},S^{[18]})
&=\dim P_{18}-\dim R_{\mathrm{dir}}(2,S^{[12]})
&&\text{by quotient dimension and (P7)}\\
&=53-51
&&\text{by (P4), (P5)}\\
&=2.
\end{align}
The remaining target-space dimension after projected constraints is
\begin{align}
d_{\mathrm{projected}}
&=|S^{[12]}|-\dim P_{18}\\
&=119-53
&&\text{by (P4), (P6)}\\
&=66.
\end{align}
Since only \(S^{[14]}\), \(S^{[16]}\), and \(S^{[18]}\) appear in the equality proof, the conclusion is a finite-chain saturation certificate rather than an unrestricted asymptotic theorem.

\textbf{Final Result.}
\[
\boxed{
P_{14}=P_{16}=P_{18},\qquad
\dim I=2,\qquad
d_{\mathrm{projected}}=66
}
\]
\hfill\(\blacksquare\)

deeper_reasoning_5:
\textbf{Claim.}
Let \(S_0\subset S_f\subset S_1\), where \(S_f\) is a filtered auxiliary set. If the filtered projected space embeds into the full projected space and the two projected spaces have the same rank, then filtering preserves all projected target-space equations:
\[
P(D,S_0,S_f)=P(D,S_0,S_1).
\]
This is the correct losslessness criterion for the paper's vertex-support filter: equality must be checked after projection to \(S_0\), not merely at the level of raw auxiliary candidates.

\textbf{Premises.}
\[
\begin{array}{ll}
(P1) & S_0\subset S_f\subset S_1.\\
(P2) & P_f:=P(D,S_0,S_f).\\
(P3) & P_1:=P(D,S_0,S_1).\\
(P4) & P_f\subseteq P_1.\\
(P5) & \dim P_f=\dim P_1.\\
(P6) & \text{If }U\subseteq V\text{ and }\dim U=\dim V,\text{ then }U=V.\\
(P7) & \text{Case I projected ranks are }2586\text{ and }2586.\\
(P8) & \text{Case II projected ranks are }347\text{ and }347.\\
(P9) & \text{Case I candidate counts are }121223\text{ before filtering and }79884\text{ after filtering.}\\
(P10) & \text{Case II candidate counts are }225570\text{ before filtering and }38322\text{ after filtering.}
\end{array}
\]

\textbf{Derivation.}
By the subspace and equal-rank criterion,
\begin{align}
P_f\subseteq P_1
\quad\text{and}\quad
\dim P_f=\dim P_1
&\Longrightarrow P_f=P_1
&&\text{by (P4), (P5), (P6)}.
\end{align}
Therefore the filtered auxiliary set is lossless exactly for the projected target-space equations under these premises:
\[
P(D,S_0,S_f)=P(D,S_0,S_1).
\]
For the two reported filter tests, the projected-rank retention fractions are
\begin{align}
\rho_{\mathrm{ret}}^{\mathrm{I}}
&=\frac{2586}{2586}=1
&&\text{by (P7)},\\
\rho_{\mathrm{ret}}^{\mathrm{II}}
&=\frac{347}{347}=1
&&\text{by (P8)}.
\end{align}
The auxiliary candidate-set reduction fractions are
\begin{align}
\rho_{\mathrm{red}}^{\mathrm{I}}
&=1-\frac{79884}{121223}\simeq0.341
&&\text{by (P9)},\\
\rho_{\mathrm{red}}^{\mathrm{II}}
&=1-\frac{38322}{225570}\simeq0.830
&&\text{by (P10)}.
\end{align}
Thus the reported filter is empirically lossless for the projected ranks in the tested cases while substantially reducing the auxiliary search space.

\textbf{Final Result.}
\[
\boxed{
P_f\subseteq P_1\text{ and }\dim P_f=\dim P_1
\Longrightarrow
P(D,S_0,S_f)=P(D,S_0,S_1)
}
\]
\[
\boxed{
\rho_{\mathrm{ret}}^{\mathrm{I}}=\rho_{\mathrm{ret}}^{\mathrm{II}}=1,\qquad
\rho_{\mathrm{red}}^{\mathrm{I}}\simeq0.341,\qquad
\rho_{\mathrm{red}}^{\mathrm{II}}\simeq0.830
}
\]
\hfill\(\blacksquare\)
